<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bengaluru</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Bangalore</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bengaluru, known until 2014 as Bangalore, is the capital of the Indian state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>. Founded in 1537 by the chief Kempe Gowda, it was held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyder-ali">Hyder Ali</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tipu-sultan">Tipu Sultan</a> and became a large British military cantonment in the 19th century. The industries and scientific institutions founded there under the princely state of Mysore laid the ground for its role as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s leading technology centre.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bengaluru.jpg" alt="Bengaluru" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gpkp · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_from_Visvesvaraya_Industrial_and_Technological_Museum_(2025)_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Bijapur annexed Bidar in 1619, and in the 1630s and 1640s its generals, among them the Maratha commander Shahaji Bhonsle, conquered much of the southern Karnataka plateau and parts of the Tamil country, taking <b>Bangalore</b> in 1638 and the fortress of Gingee in 1649.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bijapur-sultanate">Bijapur Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Bangalore</b>, a garrison and trading town, grew in importance, and Hyder Ali began the garden of Lalbagh there.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-mysore-1399-1799">Kingdom of Mysore (1399-1799) · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Its information technology industry, centred on <b>Bengaluru</b> and Hyderabad, transformed global services, and its pharmaceutical firms became major suppliers of generic medicines worldwide.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-india">Republic of India · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Krishnaraja Wodeyar IV (ruled 1894–1940, with full powers from 1902) and diwans such as M. Visvesvaraya, Mysore built the Shivanasamudra hydroelectric plant, the Krishna Raja Sagara dam, the University of Mysore and industries in <b>Bangalore</b>, which also hosted the Indian Institute of Science.</blockquote><a href="/state/mysore-state">Mysore State</a></li></ul></section>
